#1280 KS-1129 (T1). The claim: a new exported toBlockHeight(value) — null/undefined -> null, a finite number kept, a string trimmed then Number()'d with '' -> null and NaN -> null, anything else null — applied at BOTH persisting heal sites: verification.ts confirmStalePendingAnchor (the ticket's :338) and verificationV2.ts healBlobWithChainFact (:143, persisted by persistHealedAnchor at :191 — HEAL-SITES-TWO: the ticket and its gate report named only the first). The response-only sites (verification.ts :607 and :1058, verificationV2.ts :235) are deliberately untouched. RED PROOF (mandatory): verification.ts and verificationV2.ts at the parent -> the seat's five arms (arms1129.py, b29-1129-arms.out: "one per conjunct, each isolating one of the two compositions or one refusal of the coercion") red with their assertion text; restore by bytes. WRITE-REACHED: the seat says the closest sibling cell never reaches updateDocument (its fixture has no tenant_id) and that every new cell asserts the write was REACHED first, with a control that removes tenant_id and sees no write — re-run that control and say whether the cells would pass vacuously without it. TOBLOCKHEIGHT-EDGES (RUNTIME, through the REAL exported function): ' 42 ', '0x10' (Number() reads 16), '1e3', '-1', '4242.5', Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER + 2 as a string (precision loss on a BIGINT), a BigInt, Infinity, true, {} — record each result and rule which are acceptable for a block height (a negative, a fraction or a lossy integer persisted as a number is a finding the ticket did not ask about; say whether it BLOCKS). Then drive BOTH heal paths end to end (the route handler with the anchoring reply stubbed at the module boundary exactly as the cells do, updateDocument captured) with blockNumber '4242', '', 'abc' and 0: the PERSISTED blockHeight must be 4242 / the stored height / the stored height / 0. RAW-HEIGHT-KS1333 (DECLARED SCOPE): anchorStateSync's four persisting writers still write `blockHeight: anchor.blockNumber || 0` uncoerced — ticketed as KS-1333 (Backlog), and #1281 touches exactly those writers without coercing them; grade it as disclosed scope, and say whether #1280's claim ("the heal persists a number") is true of every path that PERSISTS a height after this batch (it is not: name the paths). DOCBLOCK-DISPLACED (READ): toBlockHeight's docblock was inserted between confirmStalePendingAnchor's own docblock and its function — say what a reader and the TS tooling now attach to each.

#1281 KS-1074 (T1). The claim: a new carriedForward(prior) returns {threadToken} when prior.threadToken != null (NOT truthiness — the MINOR-2 item), else {}; spread FIRST into all five rebuild writers (markDocumentAnchorFailed, the poller's confirmed and simulated writers, reconcile's confirmed and simulated writers); simulatedTxRef deliberately NOT carried (SIMULATED-NOT-CARRIED, asserted by a cell); anchoredAt deliberately NOT carried (KS-520's fail-closed contract). CARRY-FIVE-WRITERS: the drafter READ 5 `updateDocument(` calls, 5 `blockchain: {` literals and 5 `...carriedForward(` spreads in the file at head — re-count, and prove each writer by its OWN cell (the seat: "Six red arms, one per writer plus the truthiness guard, each reddening exactly its own cell"). RED PROOF (mandatory): anchorStateSync.ts at the parent -> the seat's arms (arms1074.py, b29-1074-arms.out) red, each on exactly its own cell, with the assertion text; at the parent "6 of the 8 cells are RED" — re-measure; restore by bytes. RUNTIME (the REAL module, documentRepo's updateDocument and getDocument stubbed at the module boundary, the anchoring reply stubbed): for each writer, a prior blob carrying threadToken 'tt', '' and 0 -> the written blob carries it (the != null rule); a prior blob with no threadToken -> the written blob has NO threadToken key at all (not undefined, not null — read the serialised JSON); spread ORDER — the carried key is spread FIRST, so a writer's own literal key of the same name would win: say whether any writer names threadToken itself. READ-PER-WRITE: the poller now calls getDocument() before each updateDocument; a token minted BETWEEN that read and the write is still erased — measure it with a stub that sets the token after the read resolves and before the write, and rule it (a race the ticket names or a new finding; it does not block by default — RULE WHETHER IT BLOCKS). Name the extra read per poll iteration (a second DB round trip per write) and say whether it matters.

THE KS-730 SET (KS730-THREE-TOGETHER). #1282, #1283 and #1284 are the three files of ONE ticket and share ONE shape: a local fail500(res, context, err) that logs `logger.error(context, {error: message})` and answers the constant `{success:false, error:{code:'INTERNAL_ERROR', message:'Internal server error'}}`, each call site with its own route context. grade each of #1282, #1283 and #1284 on its own AND the three together on END_TREE: at each single head the other two files still carry the off-production ternary (the drafter READ it: every head leaves the other two files' ternaries in place), so only develop + all three clears originate's share of the class. KS-730 stays In Progress after all three: its title counts 71 handlers "(api-gateway leaks it TWICE …)" and these three PRs cover originate's 65 — re-sweep originate/src on END_TREE for `err.message` in any response body and name every survivor by route (the four KS-1334 sites below are expected survivors). The three helpers are three COPIES (one per file): say whether a shared helper was the better shape and whether the copies are byte-identical (READ), without grading the duplication as a defect of any one PR. NODE-ENV-MATRIX (RUNTIME, per PR, through the REAL router over 127.0.0.1 port 0, the service call stubbed to throw a sentinel LEAK message): NODE_ENV unset, 'development', 'demo', 'test' and 'production' — at the PARENT the sentinel is in the 500 body for every value but 'production'; at the HEAD it is in NO body for any value, and IS in the logger call; the control is the parent run, which must be able to show the leak. LOG-CONTENT and LOG-SINK: fail500 now logs err.message for routes that never logged before — READ utils/logger.ts (winston; Console always, logs/error.log + combined.log in production; its header comment says "Errors >= 500 are automatically persisted to system_errors table" — find whether ANY code does that, because if it does, #1282's GET /api/system-errors would serve the very message